      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Name clash: culinary sage is Salvia officinalis (own entry, row 67); tender border salvias (S. x jamensis, S. 'Amistad') are other plants. Watering: RHS guide says drought tolerant once established, allow established plants to dry out a bit between waterings, dislike continually wet soil; pots dry out faster and must not be waterlogged; RHS profile moist but well-drained, drought tolerant once established; NC State average moist well-drained soil, drought-tolerant. let_top_dry is the agent's mapping; 7 days is a guess. Light: full sun, tolerates light partial shade (RHS profile), full sun to partial shade (NC State). Size: RHS profile max height 0.5 to 1 m, spread 0.1 to 0.5 m; NC State 1.5 to 3 ft by 0.5 to 2 ft; 90 by 60 cm is the agent's pick from the overlap, hence from_knowledge_verify. Growth: fast is the RHS genus guide's wording ('fast-growing plants'), applied to the species. Hardiness: RHS H7 (hardier than -20 C), NC State zones 4 to 8; no temperature stored. Pruning: cut back spent spikes, cut to near ground level in July for a second flush (RHS). Toxicity: ASPCA has no S. nemorosa entry; the only ASPCA sage entry is S. officinalis (non-toxic to dogs, cats, horses), so severity 0 is a genus-level reading, weak, listed under pets unknown. NC State page has no pet tags. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
